Ask five different coaching websites when NExT is launching and you'll get five different years. 2027. 2028. "2028-29." One site says it's already supposed to apply to this year's graduates. We went looking for the NMC notice that any of these numbers comes from. We didn't find one.

Here's what we found instead. A regulation, a cancelled mock test, a deferral letter, and three years of near-total silence from the body that's supposed to be running this exam.

## What NExT was supposed to replace

NExT, the National Exit Test, was built to fold three separate things MBBS students currently do, your final-year university exam, the NEET-PG entrance test, and the licensing exam that lets you practice, into one exam. The legal basis is Section 15 of the National Medical Commission Act, 2019. The plan has two parts. Step 1 is a theory exam, usually described as covering six subject papers with around 540 MCQs total, though the exact numbers have shifted across different versions of the proposal, since the NMC never published a final, locked syllabus. Step 2 is a practical and clinical exam, taken after a 12-month internship.

That second part matters. Until Step 2 happens, nobody passes NExT. The exam was designed to replace your license to practice, not just a paper qualification.

## The regulations were notified, then cancelled within two weeks

The National Exit Test Regulations, 2023 were notified in the Gazette of India on June 28, 2023. AIIMS Delhi scheduled a mock test for July 28, 2023, to test the system before a real rollout.

The mock test never happened. On July 11, 2023, the NMC issued a public notice deferring the exam entirely, on the advice of the Union Health Ministry, "till further directions." Students and doctors had already raised a specific legal objection. Applying NExT to the 2019 MBBS batch would mean testing students under a law passed after they'd already started their degree. The Health Ministry responded by setting up a committee, chaired by the Director General of Health Services, on October 3, 2023, to examine implementation and preparedness. A separate seven-member committee was formed to check whether states and colleges were ready for the 2020 batch.

{% statCallout
   number="3+ years"
   label="Time since the NExT Regulations were gazetted, with no exam held and no final launch date confirmed in any NMC notice (NMC, 2023-2026)." /%}

## What's happened since, in the record we could find

By October 2024, reporting based on an RTI response showed there was still no clarity on whether the 2020 batch would be required to take NExT, and the final draft of the regulations still hadn't been released.

The clearest recent signal is from August 2026. The NMC put out a draft amendment, the Registration of Medical Practitioners and Licence to Practice Medicine (Amendment) Regulations, 2026, which keeps the existing FMGE in place as the qualifying exam for foreign medical graduates until NExT comes into force. That's the opposite of what the 2023 regulations originally said, which was that FMGE would be replaced by NExT. In the same reporting, NMC Chairperson Dr. Abhijat Sheth was quoted saying NExT may not be implemented for a couple more years while the Commission works out the model. As of this writing, NMC's own "What's New" notices page carries nothing about NExT at all.

{% callout type="summary" %}
The clearest official statement on NExT's timeline, as of now, is that there isn't one. The NMC is writing new rules around the assumption that NExT isn't coming soon, not announcing when it will.
{% /callout %}

## Where the specific years come from

None of this stopped dozens of exam-prep and study-abroad sites from publishing confident dates. Searching for NExT's status turns up pages citing 2027, 2028, "2028-29," and 2029, several of them attributed loosely to "NMC officials" with no notice or quote attached. We could not trace a single one of these specific years back to an NMC document. The honest answer, based on what the NMC has published, is that nobody outside the Commission currently knows when NExT launches, including, it seems, several of the sites confidently telling you a year.

## What this means for you right now

If you're a current MBBS student, nothing about your exams has changed. You still sit your university's final professional exams. If you're aiming for postgraduate seats, NEET-PG is still the entrance exam. If you studied abroad, FMGE is still the exam standing between you and an Indian licence, and the August 2026 draft confirms that stays true until NExT is formally notified as live, not simply gazetted.

The one real risk in all this is studying for an exam that doesn't exist yet instead of the one in front of you. It's worth knowing NExT is coming, eventually, in some form. It's not worth losing sleep over a 2028 date nobody at the NMC has confirmed.

## Where GalenAI fits

The pattern in this story is the same one worth watching for anywhere. A confident, specific-sounding claim spreads faster than the boring original document it's supposedly based on. Nobody checked the primary notice before publishing "NExT in 2028." That's a habit worth building now, with the syllabus you're being tested on.

